NANOCHEMISTRY

1. Meaning of nanoscale

Nanomaterials contain structures with dimensions typically in the range of about 1–100 nm. At this scale, materials can show properties different from their bulk forms because surface area becomes very large relative to volume and quantum-scale effects may become important.

2. Properties

Nanomaterials can have:
• high surface area;
• unusual optical properties;
• enhanced catalytic activity;
• altered electrical conductivity;
• distinctive mechanical properties.

3. Catalysis

A high surface-area-to-volume ratio provides many active sites. Nanoparticles can therefore be highly effective catalysts, although stability, aggregation and recovery must be considered.

4. Applications

Applications include:
• catalysts;
• sensors;
• targeted drug-delivery research;
• water purification;
• coatings;
• electronics;
• energy storage;
• solar technologies;
• antimicrobial surfaces.

5. Environmental and health considerations

Nanoparticles may behave differently from larger particles in biological systems and the environment. Safe design requires assessment of exposure, persistence, mobility, toxicity and disposal.

6. Characterisation

Nanomaterials may be characterised using techniques that determine particle size, surface structure, composition, morphology and optical/electronic properties.

7. Sustainable nanochemistry

The usefulness of nanomaterials should be balanced against energy required for production, solvent use, resource demands, end-of-life handling and possible environmental release.

PRACTICAL CHEMISTRY AND EXAMINATION SKILLS

The Form 5-6 Chemistry syllabus explicitly integrates practical work, safety, measurement, data analysis and problem solving. The assessment structure includes theory and a practical examination involving qualitative analysis, quantitative analysis and planning/design.

1. Laboratory safety

Wear appropriate eye protection and protective clothing. Know the hazards of acids, alkalis, oxidising agents, flammable solvents, toxic gases and heavy-metal compounds.

Never pipette by mouth. Add concentrated acid to water slowly with stirring when dilution is required. Handle volatile or toxic substances in suitable ventilation. Dispose of chemical waste according to its hazard class.

2. Measurement and accuracy

Use the appropriate apparatus:
• burette for accurate variable volumes;
• volumetric pipette for a fixed accurate volume;
• volumetric flask for preparing a solution to a fixed volume;
• balance for mass;
• thermometer/probe for temperature.

Read scales at eye level to avoid parallax. Record all readings with appropriate precision.

Accuracy refers to closeness to the true value. Precision refers to closeness of repeated measurements to each other.

3. Titration technique

Rinse burette with the solution it will contain. Rinse pipette with the solution being transferred. Condition the conical flask appropriately without contaminating the measured aliquot.

Remove air bubbles from the burette tip. Record initial and final burette readings. Add titrant rapidly at first and dropwise near the endpoint. Swirl continuously.

Use concordant titres and calculate the mean according to the examination instructions.

4. Qualitative analysis

A qualitative analysis identifies ions or compounds using characteristic reactions.

Examples:
• carbonate + acid → CO2, which can turn limewater milky;
• chloride + acidified AgNO3 → white AgCl;
• bromide → cream AgBr;
• iodide → yellow AgI;
• sulfate + suitable barium reagent under acidic conditions → white BaSO4;
• ammonium salts release ammonia with strong alkali on warming.

For cations, observations may include precipitate colour, solubility in excess reagent and complex formation.

5. Gas tests

Hydrogen gives a squeaky pop with a lighted splint.
Oxygen relights a glowing splint.
Carbon dioxide turns limewater milky.
Ammonia turns damp red litmus blue and forms dense white fumes with hydrogen chloride under suitable conditions.

6. Planning investigations

A strong plan identifies:
• independent variable;
• dependent variable;
• controlled variables;
• apparatus;
• procedure;
• measurements;
• repeats;
• safety;
• method of processing data;
• expected relationship.

Only change one independent variable at a time where the design requires controlled comparison.

7. Graphs and data

Plot independent variable on the x-axis and dependent variable on the y-axis unless scientific reasoning requires otherwise. Use sensible scales. Plot points accurately and draw an appropriate best-fit line or curve.

Gradient = change in y / change in x.

Anomalous results should be identified and investigated rather than automatically deleted.

8. Evaluation

Discuss systematic and random errors separately. Suggest realistic improvements, such as insulation, improved temperature measurement, more precise volumetric apparatus, increased repeats or better control of variables.

9. Practical redox

In redox titrations, balance the ionic equation first. Determine the electron ratio and use it in the mole calculation.

10. Practical energetics

Minimise heat loss, use adequate insulation and stir consistently. If a temperature-time graph is used, extrapolation can compensate for heat exchange with the surroundings.

11. Examination discipline

For observations, state what is actually seen: colour change, precipitate, gas evolution, temperature change, dissolution or effervescence. Do not write an explanation where an observation is requested.

For conclusions, link evidence to a chemical principle. For calculations, show equations, substitutions, units and sensible significant figures.
